c57546fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293c2bba-fb9f-5a3b-a596-e4fc57546fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401033} :answer-field/id #uuid "61740183-1d83-54ca-8ff3-51d0ccdbf570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd646452-89ff-5f38-8817-208b877e41c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-15144" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }+\\theta {e}^{\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2}){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ea08a1-9720-53ab-801d-2d2c63e34eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{2} + 32\\sqrt{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089604} {:answer/id #uuid "0f00cec2-f71a-52a2-a3e2-5adbf8637e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2} - 32\\sqrt{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089605} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8d6a71-1856-5258-95e4-1fb0d98010b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{2} + 8\\sqrt{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089606} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba7f16c-7354-5413-833e-70fae3f6f9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{3} + 8\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089607} {:answer/id #uuid "6fccf4cd-8f6e-5638-8dbb-e07f13b11d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{3} - 4\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089608}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1754a1-8e2a-5496-a250-3d29835d9604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089603}] :question/id #uuid "3f02ee97-98c3-587d-83d6-df4090482f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-15148" :question/problem "What is $[2cis\\left(\\frac{π}{24}\\right)]^{6}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c02db39-1323-5178-823d-035cbc0e974b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273319} {:answer/id #uuid "76f538de-df54-5fad-9a26-4e6bcab7eb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273399} {:answer/id #uuid "7f93ecb1-ea78-5b92-86af-7eaf211452f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273483} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a2a416-fa2b-57e0-ae21-9414bcd449ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273807} {:answer/id #uuid "ebffd150-b312-515d-a26e-013ddad9564b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebffd150-b312-515d-a26e-013ddad9564b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d36b979-5ca7-502e-b3f6-581c418d1ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d674e5dc-8a57-5368-b53e-8363dba234a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15185" :question/problem "![](images/80/80577a585741748702b61596db84983592ecbc6c01fca78af033bf82dbe62ad2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82550668-6db1-54fe-81a6-7decbfb26078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089742} {:answer/id #uuid "959a79f2-f97e-535a-b683-d17bf7bf464a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089743} {:answer/id #uuid "ae215cc0-ff58-55c2-865e-b974884c81ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089744} {:answer/id #uuid "ea754704-1fa6-5af4-a0de-d3662d903bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089745} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc06628-b248-575c-9b36-e35a33f71da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089746}] :answer-field/id #uuid "01598c6d-608c-5ac2-9e15-33de5a7f22a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089741}] :question/id #uuid "12f3fee8-ceae-59dc-b214-42d5a85d7c68" :question/math-academy-id "q-17112" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the polar curve $r = \\sin (5θ)$ at $θ = \\frac{π}{5}$ given that\n$y^{′}(θ) = 5\\cos (5θ)\\sin θ + \\sin (5θ)\\cos θ, x^{′}(θ) = 5\\cos (5θ)\\cos θ - \\sin (5θ)\\sin θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868edb60-e59c-50bd-a3b1-3e31f56dc01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\cos θ - \\textrm{i}\\sin θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089802} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c60aa2-0b70-58cc-ad3b-c99485a3fc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos \\left(\\frac{θ}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{θ}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089803} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e91ff3-8ea0-59d4-a9f3-f3712d916229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\cos θ + \\textrm{i}\\sin θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089804} {:answer/id #uuid "68e6bcfb-6a3a-5db5-ada3-4cbfab7652d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (4θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (4θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089805} {:answer/id #uuid "6c08ac70-d8b7-58f2-99f3-92055001827e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (4θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (4θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089806}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd0e43c-dd58-5147-b2f4-27bb01633600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089801}] :question/id #uuid "a8df3479-1465-57b6-867e-a89ad33870c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-17322" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $[\\cos θ + \\textrm{i}\\sin θ]^{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25dbdfef-762b-5e3a-9a87-f25a7d801f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444337} {:answer/id #uuid "3874f5d4-8dd6-5b8f-8a1b-a542d4134288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444339} {:answer/id #uuid "7b602e5a-cdcf-5c39-85aa-d6ee24446194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444347} {:answer/id #uuid "874aab85-54f1-59bd-809e-d0fbd59d4f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444348} {:answer/id #uuid "99948bc6-a92f-552f-8040-f4159eba3490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3874f5d4-8dd6-5b8f-8a1b-a542d4134288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444339} :answer-field/id #uuid "68afe63f-78cb-575a-8735-eb0f15a48444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f08571df-c501-5a24-a4f9-9bde44801775" :question/math-academy-id "q-18082" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a9c8d8-68c5-587e-838a-4a7f7a9f44bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284840} {:answer/id #uuid "269e3329-f871-5045-88f0-2b40179a9752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285128} {:answer/id #uuid "725d36f9-f189-56dd-98a0-db99c79bb457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285840} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b81c32-7857-5a5e-80e1-2d918aea9dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286425} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd63f8c-9c63-51fb-b9ec-35a75373c9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725d36f9-f189-56dd-98a0-db99c79bb457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285840} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0b2d2e-e7a6-5a1c-bd3e-245bfcd76f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc963679-7e9a-589a-a899-b98503fae964" :question/math-academy-id "q-20414" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f00d8bd6cbd1f113df7f023e161e4d79e2f4a81157d1ac9a8ab65e70b4959221.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$" :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4da550-43d1-5bf0-ba07-2d9c8866fa45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528557} {:answer/id #uuid "87cb5c61-72c1-5207-ac68-6c4fc40a404e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528625} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7ba2fb-9f38-56bc-8fe5-e96e6dbabc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +8}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528675} {:answer/id #uuid "a23714c3-4f78-5ba1-a107-d1e73692eea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528692} {:answer/id #uuid "f974103f-b624-52cf-acc4-f3366c464e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4da550-43d1-5bf0-ba07-2d9c8866fa45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528557} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d48976e-ecd9-52fe-8371-edaabd05c065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb2c5d25-e3ba-5c5d-9450-482e68e4a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-20640" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a83949bcdf4ed8d99cac451d6db21986c24819d1601578890f86fe3df99666a.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a99190-855b-5a97-9d34-03834cbd05d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a99190-855b-5a97-9d34-03834cbd05d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290770} :answer-field/id #uuid "0860cd97-7df0-517b-85eb-0477ff1f4fa0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a1bcff-e6be-5276-bd40-a5e193187ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1bcff-e6be-5276-bd40-a5e193187ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9197374-8ffc-5179-b8a7-723e02b609fd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55659377-1a68-5cde-bfb5-257e09e1e167" :question/math-academy-id "q-255772" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-({x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1.5)-(1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5) \\\\  & =2.391\\,802….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2(2.391\\,802…)-(2.391\\,802…)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2.391\\,802…) \\\\  & =2.697\\,840….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.392$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.698,$ rounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80afb707-adb7-5d2a-ba2f-455d5a7248ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090365} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ce7f64-7079-5844-bc3d-bca849694c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090366} {:answer/id #uuid "06caa734-7da6-561e-abbe-02e27ccf0eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090367} {:answer/id #uuid "48a7fba2-de12-5e32-be67-3437e0d6e394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{114}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090368} {:answer/id #uuid "4b33868d-73f4-5a5c-8210-eab21bb5714b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{252}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090369}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c10149a4-2ac0-5507-b798-cdbb6447ae88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71fb5123-a304-5cfe-b3ff-c0a8f7acc21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{114}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373026} {:answer/id #uuid "79e1b473-fbb9-5d67-bf0d-e43d40835360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373027} {:answer/id #uuid "317ebda9-9813-536e-b2ad-8c5b9aaa9fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{252}{729}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373028} {:answer/id #uuid "bbcc4da7-523c-5b7b-b5e4-ebac5d315919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373029} {:answer/id #uuid "1446d8eb-8576-5ca4-9278-5496ce37568c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbcc4da7-523c-5b7b-b5e4-ebac5d315919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373029} :answer-field/id #uuid "1656cce0-af78-56c8-8d77-e4788e57998f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e7df8e8-e8b3-58f8-bc82-f13b1d62b255" :question/math-academy-id "q-39913" :question/problem "The second term of a geometric series is $\\frac{1}{12}$ and the third term is $\\frac{1}{36}.$ What is the sum of the first $6$ terms?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{36}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{12} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4},N=6$ and $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{6})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{6}})}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{3}^{6}-1}{{3}^{6}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{728}{729}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908fe6e3-f